=== AUDIT_EVIDENCE ===

A1 — fsm_transitions schema (mig 000187 lines 53-65):
    CREATE TABLE fsm_transitions (
      id         BIGSERIAL PRIMARY KEY,
      vehicle_id BIGINT NOT NULL,
      ts         TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,
      fsm_name   TEXT NOT NULL,
      from_state TEXT,           -- nullable
      to_state   TEXT NOT NULL,
      trigger    TEXT,           -- nullable
      details    JSONB           -- nullable
    );
    CREATE INDEX fsm_transitions_vehicle_ts ON fsm_transitions (vehicle_id, ts DESC);
    CREATE INDEX fsm_transitions_fsm_ts     ON fsm_transitions (fsm_name, ts DESC);

  Confirmed by `Get-Content migrations/000187_fsm_live.up.sql` and
  cross-checked against internal/database/schema_test.go:111-117 which
  pins these exact columns. Prior table from mig 000047 + the
  `fsm_type` column added by mig 000144 were dropped by 000187's
  DROP TABLE IF EXISTS fsm_transitions CASCADE preamble (line 42).

A2 — Schema vs Decision #5 mismatch:
  Decision #5 says columns trigger_field, trigger_value. ACTUAL schema
  has columns trigger TEXT, details JSONB. Mismatch resolved per
  prompt's escape hatch:

    trigger_field <- trigger
    trigger_value <- details ->> trigger    (JSONB lookup at trigger key)

  fsmAction.Execute (internal/api/fsm_handler.go:92-100) writes the
  details map with the trigger field name as the key (e.g.
  trigger='Gear', details={"Gear":"D",...}), so the JSONB lookup
  yields the value of the field that fired the transition. NULL-safe:
  PG returns NULL when trigger is NULL or details is NULL or the key
  is absent. Repo scans into `*string` (VehicleStateTransition.TriggerValue).

